        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Do Stud Finders Actually Work?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most electronic stud finders sense a change in capacitance, magnetic finders grab the steel fasteners in the wall, and radar imagers build a picture from reflected radio signals. Each approach reads the wall differently, which is why each one is strong on some walls and unreliable on others.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does a single-sensor finder read the wall?</h3>

          <p>
            Capacitance is the way the wall holds an electric field. Where a stud sits behind the drywall, that strip of wall is denser than the hollow bay beside it, and the tool picks up the difference. A <strong>single-sensor</strong> finder compares each reading against a baseline it records the moment you press the button. That design explains its two classic failures: calibrate over a stud and the baseline is wrong from the start, and slide too fast and the sensor misses the change altogether.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why is a multi-sensor array easier to trust?</h3>

          <p>
            A <strong>multi-sensor array</strong>, the layout Franklin made popular, places a row of sensors side by side, and each reads its own patch of wall at the same instant. Instead of comparing against a stored baseline, the tool compares the sensors with one another. There is no calibration step, and the answer appears as a band of lit LEDs spanning the full width of the stud. That picture is far easier to read and much harder to misinterpret.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do magnetic finders and radar imagers differ?</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Magnetic</strong> finders use no electronics at all. They latch onto the steel screws or nails that hold the wall to the framing, and since every stud carries a vertical line of fasteners, two hits stacked in a column mark the stud. <strong>Radar imagers</strong> such as the Walabot send radio signals into the wall and turn the reflections into an image, so pipes, cables and studs show up as separate shapes rather than a single beep.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is each design's blind spot?</h3>

          <p>
            Every design has one. Capacitive tools are thrown off by dense plaster, damp walls, foil-backed insulation and heavy coverings such as thick textured paint. Magnets are slow wherever fasteners are few and far between. Radar costs much more and asks you to interpret what you see. Knowing the weak point of the tool in your hand is most of the skill in using it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How deep can a stud finder really see?</h3>

          <p>
            Less deep than the box suggests. The sensing depth printed on the package is a best case: a maker that lists three-quarters of an inch in standard mode and an inch and a half in deep mode is describing a dry, even wall. Real walls with several coats of paint, a skim coat of joint compound or a layer of wallpaper read shallower. If your wall is thicker than usual, choose a tool whose rated depth clears it with room to spare, not one that barely reaches.
          </p>

