        <div id="by-wall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Stud Finder Works Best on Your Wall Type?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A multi-sensor array is the best fit for drywall, a magnet is the best fit for lath and plaster, and a metal-mode scanner is the tool for steel studs and masonry. The wall decides more than the feature list does, so identify what you are drilling into before you choose.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Standard drywall</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Half-inch drywall</strong> over wood studs is the easy case, and nearly any decent tool will find the framing. A multi-sensor array is the fastest and the least prone to error. Five-eighths drywall, which you often find on garage walls shared with the house and on some ceilings, still sits comfortably inside the range of every pick above.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lath and plaster</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Lath and plaster</strong>, common in houses built before the 1950s, is where electronic finders run into trouble. Strips of wood lath run horizontally across the studs, and the plaster keys squeezed between them vary in thickness, so a capacitive tool sees density changes everywhere it goes. A strong magnet that finds the lath nails is often the most dependable method. Multi-sensor arrays handle plaster better than single-sensor tools, but they are not foolproof here.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Steel studs</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Steel studs</strong> turn up in condos, contractor-finished basements and commercial spaces. A magnet will stick straight to the studs themselves, and a scanner with a metal mode will find them clearly. Keep in mind that steel framing calls for different fasteners, such as toggle bolts or snap toggles, because wood screws get a poor grip in thin sheet metal.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tile, paneling and masonry</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Tile, paneling and masonry</strong> each need their own approach. Tile over backer board often defeats capacitive sensors because of the added thickness, so hunt for fastener heads with a magnet or switch to a deep-scan mode. Paneling usually sits on furring strips or straight on the studs, and its nails line up with the framing behind it. Block and concrete walls have no studs at all; there the job is finding rebar, conduit and pipe before you drill, which calls for a scanner with a metal mode.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Ceilings</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Ceilings</strong> work the same way as walls, with joists or trusses usually set on 16- or 24-inch centers. Scanning overhead is awkward, so a multi-sensor array that answers in a single placement is a genuine advantage. Joists run in one direction across a room, often parallel to the shorter wall. A ceiling fan or heavy light fixture must hang from a fan-rated box fastened to framing or to a listed brace, never from the drywall alone.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">OLD HOUSE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">On plaster, believe the magnet before the beeper.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">When an electronic finder lights up all over a plaster wall, put it down and pick up a strong magnet. Locate a column of lath nails, then drill one small test hole to confirm before you hang anything heavy.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="framing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Far Apart Are Studs in a Wall?
          </h2>

          <p>
            In most North American houses, studs sit 16 inches on center, which means 16 inches from the middle of one stud to the middle of the next. Some newer homes and many garages use 24-inch spacing, and older houses can be irregular. Once you have found one stud you trust, measuring 16 inches either way predicts the next one quickly, and the finder confirms it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How wide is a stud?</h3>

          <p>
            A standard stud is a nominal two-by-four, and its face, the side you drill into, actually measures one and a half inches across. That leaves a target of three-quarters of an inch on each side of center. Hitting the middle matters: a screw that lands near one edge tends to split the wood or exit the side, and then it holds almost nothing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where does framing cluster?</h3>

          <p>
            Framing gathers in predictable spots. Door and window openings nearly always have studs on both sides, often doubled, with a header across the top. Corners carry extra framing. Outlet and switch boxes are usually nailed to the side of a stud, so a stud typically runs along one edge of the box. Tapping helps too: a hollow sound points to an empty bay, while a duller sound means something solid is behind the wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What are blocking and fire stops?</h3>

          <p>
            Blocking and fire stops are short horizontal pieces fitted between studs, which some codes require partway up taller walls. A stud finder may show one as a horizontal band. They can be handy anchor points for a shelf, but do not mistake one for a vertical stud when you are planning a long row of screws.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do exterior walls hide more?</h3>

          <p>
            Usually, yes. Exterior walls often hold extra studs around windows, diagonal bracing in older construction, and sheathing nails you can sometimes spot from outside. Interior partition walls tend to be simpler, but a wall behind a kitchen or bathroom carries supply lines and drains. Before drilling, think about what sits in the room on the other side and plan your holes to stay clear of the obvious runs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="method" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How Do You Use a Stud Finder Correctly?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clear the wall, fit a fresh battery, scan slowly and flat, and check your result at more than one height. Most bad readings come from rushing one of those steps rather than from the tool itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Prepare the wall and the tool</h3>

          <p>
            Take down pictures, move furniture out of the way, and keep your free hand off the wall, since your own body can disturb a capacitive reading. Take off rings and watches if your model is sensitive. Check that the battery is fresh, because a weak one is a frequent cause of erratic results.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Scan with a single-sensor tool</h3>

          <p>
            Set the tool flat on the wall, press and hold to calibrate, then slide it slowly and horizontally without lifting or tipping it. Mark the first edge it reports. Keep going past that edge, calibrate again on the far side, slide back the opposite way and mark the second edge. The center of the stud lies halfway between your two marks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Scan with a multi-sensor array</h3>

          <p>
            An array is simpler: press it flat against the wall and mark the section that lights up. There is no calibration to get wrong, so you can lift it and set it down anywhere along the wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Check at two or three heights</h3>

          <p>
            Scan the same stud near the floor, at waist height and at eye level. A real stud produces readings that line up vertically, while a pipe, a duct or a thick patch of plaster usually does not. Painter's tape lets you mark freely without leaving pencil lines on a finished wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cross-check against framing clues</h3>

          <p>
            When readings disagree, step back and compare them with what the house tells you: the 16-inch rhythm from a known corner, the side of an outlet box, the fastener line a magnet finds. Two independent methods that agree are worth more than any single beep. Before hanging a television or a cabinet, confirm the stud with the steps in the next section.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mark without marring the finish</h3>

          <p>
            Mark lightly and think about the finished look. A pencil tick inside the area a bracket will cover vanishes once the mount goes up. For art and shelves on painted walls, a strip of low-tack painter's tape run horizontally across the scan zone lets you mark as much as you like and peel the evidence away afterward.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="confirm" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Do You Confirm a Stud Before Drilling?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drill a small test hole. No stud finder deserves blind faith when a heavy load is involved, and the cheapest insurance is a very thin finish nail or a pilot hole of an eighth of an inch or less, placed at your mark where the mount will hide it. Solid wood behind the drywall means you have a stud. If the bit breaks through into empty space, shift over slightly and try again.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How do you find the edges?</h3>

          <p>
            Use the same small bit to probe at your center mark and three-quarters of an inch to either side. Solid wood across that whole span tells you that you are centered. If one side goes hollow, move your holes toward the other side so the screws land in the middle of the framing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should come out of the hole?</h3>

          <p>
            Watch what the pilot hole produces. Clean wood shavings mean a stud. White dust followed by nothing means you passed through the drywall into an empty bay. Metal resistance means a steel stud, a nail plate or a pipe, so stop drilling at once and investigate before going any further.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What speeds up the rest of the job?</h3>

          <p>
            Once the stud is confirmed, a <a href="/best-laser-level/">laser level</a> makes the remaining work quick: project a level line across the wall, mark the second stud on that same line, and the bracket or shelf goes up straight on the first attempt.
          </p>

